<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick Barbarossa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1122">1122</a>–<a href="/years/1190">1190</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick Barbarossa was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> in the twelfth century. He sought to assert imperial authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, clashing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> cities, and he drowned in 1190 while leading an army on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>. He became a legendary figure in the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa.jpg" alt="Frederick Barbarossa" width="256" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Montecappio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_I._Barbarossa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick was born in mid-December 1122, probably at Hagenau, the son of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> family and Judith of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> of the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a>. Descent from the two leading families of the empire made him an acceptable candidate to the princes who elected the king. He could neither read nor write and did not speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> until later in life. In 1147 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>, and after his father's death that April he succeeded him as Duke of Swabia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When his uncle Conrad III died in February 1152, Frederick and the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> both said that the dying king had handed him the royal insignia. The princes chose Frederick at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> on 4 March 1152, and he was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> on 9 March. He sought to restore the empire to the position it held under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and Otto the Great. He made six expeditions into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The first, in 1154–55, brought the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortona">Tortona</a> and the suppression of the republican commune of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-of-brescia">Arnold of Brescia</a>, who was hanged.</p><p>The Diet of Roncaglia in 1158 began a long struggle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombard-league">Lombard League</a> opposed him, and the cities beat him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnano">Battle of Legnano</a> on 29 May 1176, where he was wounded and for a time believed dead. In the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1177 he was formally reconciled with Alexander. His use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> gave him trained lawyers to administer his lands, and from 1165 he pursued policies to encourage trade; the number of mints in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> rose ninefold in the reigns of Frederick and his son Henry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1188 at the Diet of Mainz Frederick took the crusader's vow. On the advice of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> he followed a shortcut along the Saleph River, and he drowned there on 10 June 1190. Accounts differ: one says he swam across against advice, another that he was thrown from his horse and drowned weighed down by armour. Most of his army abandoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> before reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>.</p><p>Legend says he sleeps with his knights in a cave in the Kyffhäuser mountains and will wake to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The nickname Barbarossa, Red Beard, was first used by the Florentines in 1298. In the 19th and early 20th centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Risorgimento</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> and the Nazi movement each used him as a symbol.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick Barbarossa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince Levon II, who acceded in 1187, changed that: with the support of the Holy Roman Emperors <b>Frederick Barbarossa</b> and Henry VI he was crowned in the cathedral of Tarsus on 6 January 1198, the Armenian Christmas, by the Catholicos Gregory VI Abirad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…a stem duchy of the Empire it passed between families: the Ottonian kings gave it to relatives, the Welfs held it from 1070 with interruptions, and in 1180 <b>Frederick Barbarossa</b> deposed Henry the Lion and granted the duchy to Otto of Wittelsbach, whose family ruled Bavaria until 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>As Polish power fragmented after Bolesław’s death in 1138, the dukes turned towards the German princes: after defeat by Saxon and Danish forces at Verchen in 1164 they submitted to Henry the Lion, and in 1181 Bogislaw I received his duchy as an imperial fief from <b>Frederick Barbarossa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1176 he refused Emperor <b>Frederick Barbarossa</b> military help in Italy, shortly before the emperor’s defeat at Legnano, and Saxon princes then brought complaints against him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1156 Emperor <b>Frederick Barbarossa</b> gave the palatinate to his half-brother Conrad of Hohenstaufen, who built a territorial base on the Rhine and the lower Neckar around Heidelberg and Alzey, together with advocacies over church lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamburg celebrates its port's birthday every May on the strength of a charter of <b>Frederick Barbarossa</b> dated 1189 that historians have shown to be a thirteenth-century forgery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Haguenau, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in River Göksu, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.82,7.79&amp;year=1166" aria-label="Haguenau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Frederick Barbarossa"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5437 5925h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5437 5925h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.82,7.79&amp;year=1166">What was here in 1166? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
